data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","arguments":"{\"title\":\"California Weather Patterns Summary\",\"content\":\"### General Weather Patterns in California\\n- **Climate Variability**: California's climate ranges from Mediterranean along the coast to desert in the southeastern regions. Coastal areas have mild, wet winters and hot, dry summers, while inland areas experience greater temperature extremes.\\n- **Precipitation**: The north typically receives more rainfall, primarily from November to April.\\n- **Temperature**: Moderated along the coast but more extreme inland.\\n\\n### Specific Weather Phenomena\\n- **Droughts**: Frequent and can last several years, impacting water supply and increasing wildfire risk.\\n- **Wildfires**: Becoming more frequent and severe, particularly in dry and windy conditions.\\n- **El Niño Events**: Bring increased winter rainfall, often causing flooding and mudslides.\\n\\n### Recent Changes and Trends\\n- **Climate Change Impacts**: Rising average temperatures contribute to more extreme weather and intensified droughts.\\n- **Precipitation Trends**: Increased intensity of rainfall events, with longer dry spells in between.\\n- **Sea-Level Rise**: Coastal erosion and flooding threats are increasing.\\n\\nThis summary provides an overview of the diverse and changing weather patterns in California, highlighting the impact of specific phenomena and recent climate trends.\"}","item_id":"fc_0ea214dbd6290fe5006ad8ef1587d487d1b68cf965f8437837","output_index":1,"sequence_number":392}

